            <ArticleTitle>Analysis of the masticatory process of asthmatic children: Clinical and electromyographic research.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>358-64</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7162/S1809-97772012000300010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND"> The prevalence of asthma has grown considerably in recent decades, but some studies have shown stabilization of this trend. The masticatory process of asthmatic children may be altered due to asthma-related anatomo-functional changes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE"> The study objective was to determine the clinical and electromyographic characteristics of the masticatory process in asthmatic children and compare the electrical activities of their masseter and anterior temporal muscles (at rest and during maximal voluntary contraction and mastication) with those of non-asthmatic children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS"> Case study. Asthmatic and non-asthmatic groups, each consisting of 30 children of both sexes between 6 and 10 years of age, were evaluated. Mastication was evaluated clinically and electromyographically in all subjects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The masticatory process did not differ significantly between asthmatic and non-asthmatic children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS"> Although the masticatory process did not differ significantly between asthmatic and non-asthmatic children, the masticatory process of asthmatic children may be altered because of anatomical changes of Asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
